    "result_text": "I implemented both functions in `app/network.py`. `pytest -q` passes (3 tests). Those tests don't cover the new code, so I also checked it with a throwaway script against a stub transport. The requests had the right URL, the `X-Acme-Job: nightly-sync` header and huggingface_hub's own user agent, with timeouts of 5s connect and 60s for read, write and pool.\n\n**`configure_hub_http(transport=None)`** registers a client factory with `huggingface_hub.set_client_factory`, so every `HfApi` instance uses the one shared `httpx2.Client` (huggingface_hub 2.0 uses `httpx2`, not `httpx`). The client uses the given transport, sends the job header, and keeps huggingface_hub's own request hook (offline mode, request IDs, debug logging). It also still follows redirects.\n\n**Issue with the timeouts:** setting them on the client alone doesn't work. Methods like `HfApi.model_info` pass `timeout=None` on each request, and `httpx2` reads that as \"no timeout\", overriding the client default. So I added a small request hook that applies our timeouts when a request has none set. A caller who passes an explicit timeout still gets theirs; I checked this with `timeout=3`.\n\n**`current_revision(repo_id)`** returns `HfApi().model_info(repo_id, revision=\"main\").sha`. On a timeout or network error it logs a warning on `app.network` and returns `None`; anything else raises. I checked all three cases: a normal lookup returned the sha, a timeout returned `None` with the warning, and a 404 raised `RepositoryNotFoundError`.\n\nThree things to know:\n- **Transport reuse:** huggingface_hub closes the shared client after a connection error and builds a new one from the factory, and every new client reuses the same `transport` object. That's fine for a stub transport. A real `httpx2.HTTPTransport` would be closed along with the client, so pass a factory-friendly transport or leave it as `None`.\n- **Async requests aren't covered:** huggingface_hub's async client isn't shared by design, so I didn't configure it. T"
  },
